text:
Root A-TH-TH (أثث) — Lisān al-ʿArab (Ibn Manẓūr)

1. Core meaning: abundance, bulk. Al-athāth, al-athāthah, al-uthūth all denote abundance/largeness "of anything." The verb aththa (ya'aththu/ya'ithth/ya'uththu) carries the same sense. Ibn Sīdah classes it as a faʿl-pattern word; the adjective athīth and feminine athīthah follow the same root.

2. Used of plants and hair. A plant that "aththa-ed" has become thick and entangled — athīth. The same word describes copious, long hair and luxuriant vegetation. Imruʾ al-Qays uses it of hair, comparing it to a date-palm cluster heavy with fruit. A thick, bushy beard is likewise called athth/kathth (= athīthah).

3. Used of a woman's figure. A woman who "aththa-ed" has become large-hipped/full-buttocked. Al-Ṭirimmāḥ's line contrasts this with a slender waist and soft upper body — ample behind, delicate above. A woman called athīthah is described (per this entry) as full-fleshed. Ruʾbah's verse praises "heavy-hipped, plump" women — though one word in his line (al-awāʿith) is obscure and its exact sense is not given.

4. A causative use. Athtatha something = to make it smooth/level and cushioned/padded.

5. Al-athāth = wealth, goods, household furnishings. Several glosses are stacked here, without fully reconciling them:
   - abundant wealth, or simply abundance of wealth;
   - all property and goods — clothing, bedding-stuffing, or an outer wrap;
   - Ibn Duraid links it to "that which is made soft/cushioned" (i.e., padded furnishings);
   - the Qur'anic phrase "athāthan wa ri'yā" (19:74) is glossed by al-Farrāʾ and Abū Zayd simply as matāʿ, "goods/furnishings";
   - elsewhere, al-athāth is said to mean the whole of one's wealth — camels, sheep, slaves, and goods;
   - al-Farrāʾ notes al-athāth has no true singular (like matāʿ), though if forced to pluralize it one could say "three units of āthah" or "many uthut";
   - finally, it's glossed simply as the various kinds of household goods.

6. A related verb of prospering. Taʾaththatha ("to become possessed of athāth") means the man acquired wealth/good things — per al-Ṣiḥāḥ, fine possessions.

7. A personal name. Athāthah (here noted as vocalized with ḍamma, i.e., Uthāthah) is a man's name; Ibn Duraid guesses it derives from this root.

Root د-ر-أ, as treated by al-Rāghib al-Iṣfahānī in al-Mufradāt fī Gharīb al-Qurʾān.

1. Core sense. الدَّرْء (al-darʾ) is a leaning or inclining toward one of the two sides. Hence قوّمت درأه — "I straightened his lean," i.e. corrected his deviation.

2. Derived sense: pushing something away from a side. دَرَأْتُ عنه means "I repelled from his side." From this: ذو تَدَرُّؤٍ, said of a man strong at repelling his enemies; and دَارَأْتُهُ (form III), "I pushed against / warded him off."

3. Qurʾānic uses cited for this repelling sense:
   - "they repel the evil deed with the good one" (13:22)
   - "and it repels the punishment from her" (24:8)
   - "Say: then repel death from yourselves" (3:168)
   Al-Rāghib also cites a ḥadīth, "repel the ḥudūd by means of doubts," which he reads as pointing to seeking some means by which the penalty can be pushed away.

4. Grammatical analysis of فَادَّارَأْتُمْ فِيهَا (2:72). He classes it as form VI (tafāʿaltum), not form VIII. The original is تَدَارَأْتُمْ; for ease of pronunciation the tāʾ was assimilated — turned into a dāl and given sukūn — which then required a helping (conjunctive) alif at the front, producing the shape he writes as افّاعلتم.

5. Refutation of the form-VIII reading. An unnamed literary figure had analysed ادّارأتم as iftaʿaltum. Al-Rāghib lists seven objections:
   (i) ادّارأتم has eight letters, iftaʿaltum seven;
   (ii) in iftaʿaltum the letter after the conjunctive alif is a tāʾ, but he made it a dāl;
   (iii) conversely, he turned into a tāʾ what is a dāl in the actual word;
   (iv) with a sound middle radical, whatever follows the tāʾ of iftiʿāl must carry a vowel, yet here it is quiescent;
   (v) an extra letter stands between the tāʾ and the dāl here, which never happens in iftaʿaltu;
   (vi) he treated the alif as occupying the slot of the middle radical, which it is not;
   (vii) in iftaʿala two letters precede and two follow, whereas ادّارأتم has three following.

Ibn Fāris opens by saying this root is an exception to his usual method: qāf–ṭāʾ–rāʾ is not built on one underlying analogy, and its words come from separate origins, so he simply records them.

1. **Side, flank, region** — quṭr is a side or quarter; the plural aqṭār means the flanks or sides of a thing. Hence the idiom "he thrust at him and qaṭṭarahu," i.e. threw him down onto one of his two sides. Cited verse: "Salmā and her neighbours know well that none but I laid the horseman on his side."

2. **Aloes-wood** — quṭur is the aromatic wood (al-ʿūd). Cited from Ṭarafa: the people in their assembly call out asking whether what they smell is the smoke of roasting meat (qutār) or the scent of aloes-wood (quṭur).

3. **Dripping, and by extension succession** — qaṭr is the falling of water or anything else in drops. Ibn Fāris notes that here, at least, the root does yield a regular sense: the idea of one thing following another in sequence (tatābuʿ). Under this he groups:
   - qiṭār al-ibil, a string of camels tied nose-to-tail;
   - taqāṭara al-qawm, said when people arrive in successive parties — derived, he says, from the camel-string;
   - al-baʿīr al-qāṭir, the camel whose urine keeps dripping;
   - the proverb "al-infāḍ yuqaṭṭir al-jalab": when provisions run short, people string their camels together and drive them off to market;
   - qaṭirān (tar), which he suggests may be named from dripping; he analyses it as the pattern faʿilān;
   - qaṭartu al-baʿīra bi-l-hināʾ, smearing a camel with tar-ointment, with the verse "as the anointing man dripped [tar] on the tarred she-camel."

4. **Items outside that analogy** — Ibn Fāris explicitly sets these apart as not reducible to the above:
   - qiṭr, copper;
   - qaṭara fī al-arḍ, he went off into the land, departed;
   - iqṭārra al-nabāt, said of a plant that has come close to drying out.

Root س-ع-ي (s-ʿ-y), per al-Rāghib al-Iṣfahānī's Mufradāt.

Core sense: السَّعْي (al-saʿy) is swift walking — faster than an ordinary gait but short of an outright run (ʿadw). By extension it means earnest exertion or striving in some matter, and the striving may be for good or for evil alike.

Qur'anic attestations of the neutral (good-or-evil) range:
- "strove for its ruin" (2:114); "their light running before them" (66:8) — good;
- "they strive on earth for corruption" (5:64); "when he turns away, he strives on earth [to corrupt]" (2:205) — evil;
- "man shall have only that for which he strives, and his striving shall soon be seen" (53:39–40); "your striving is diverse" (92:4); "strives for it [the Hereafter] with fitting striving" and "their striving is thanked" (17:19); "no denial of reward for his striving" (21:94).

Note on usage: al-Rāghib observes that most often saʿy is applied to praiseworthy deeds, citing a verse of poetry (requiting ʿAlqama b. Saʿd for his effort).

Sense of reaching an age/capacity: in "when he reached [the age of] striving with him" (37:102), it means the boy grew old enough to pursue and work alongside [his father].

Specialized derivatives al-Rāghib lists:
- saʿy — the ritual walking between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwa;
- al-siʿāya — used specifically for (a) tale-bearing/slander, (b) collecting the ṣadaqa levy, (c) the labour by which a mukātab (contracted slave) earns his own manumission;
- al-musāʿāh — used for licentiousness/fornication;
- al-masʿāh — the pursuit of honour or noble conduct.

Finally, "those who strive against Our signs, seeking to frustrate" (34:5) is glossed: they exerted themselves to make God's revealed signs appear powerless.

Al-Rāghib treats z-k-w as growth that comes from God's blessing, in worldly and in otherworldly matters alike.

**1. Growth and blessing.** The root sense of zakāh is growth arising from God's blessing (baraka). "Zakā al-zarʿu yazkū" is said of a crop when it grows and prospers. In "which of them has the purest food" (azkā ṭaʿāman, Q 18:19), he reads the word as pointing to food that is lawful and whose after-effects are not unwholesome.

**2. Zakāh as a due given to the poor.** From this comes al-zakāh, the name for what a person gives from God's due to the poor. Al-Rāghib gives three possible reasons for the name: the hope of blessing in it, the growth of the soul in good things (tazkiya), or both together, since both goods are in it. He notes that the Qur'an pairs zakāh with prayer: "establish the prayer and give the zakāh" (Q 2:43).

**3. Purification of the soul.** Through the purity and growth (zakāʾ) of the soul a person earns praise in this world and reward in the next. It consists in seeking out what purifies oneself. The Qur'an assigns this purifying to four agents:
- the person, who acquires it: "he has succeeded who purifies it" (Q 91:9);
- God, who is its real agent: "rather, God purifies whom He wills" (Q 4:49);
- the Prophet, as the intermediary: "you cleanse them and purify them by it" (Q 9:103); "reciting Our signs to you and purifying you" (Q 2:151);
- worship, as its instrument: "tenderness from Us, and purity (zakātan)" (Q 19:13).

**4. Pure by creation.** In "a pure boy" (ghulāman zakiyyan, Q 19:19), zakiyy means made pure by creation. Al-Rāghib links this to divine choosing (ijtibāʾ): God makes some servants knowing and pure of character through divine enabling rather than study or practice, as with most prophets and messengers. Alternatively, the boy is called pure for what he would become, meaning "he will become pure."

**5. Grammar of Q 23:4.** "Those who, for zakāh, are doers" (li-l-zakāti fāʿilūn) means they perform their acts of worship so that God may purify them, or so that they may purify themselves, which al-Rāghib says is one meaning. He states that li-l-zakāh is not the grammatical object of fāʿilūn: the lām marks cause and purpose.

**6. Self-purification: deed versus word.** A person's tazkiya of himself is of two kinds:
- by deed, which is praiseworthy: "he has succeeded who purifies it" (Q 91:9); "he has succeeded who purifies himself" (Q 87:14);
- by word, as when an upright person vouches for another. Doing this for oneself is blameworthy and forbidden: "so do not declare yourselves pure" (Q 53:32). Al-Rāghib explains the prohibition as a lesson in manners, since self-praise is ugly by reason and by revealed law, and he cites a sage who, asked what is not good even when true, answered: "a man's praise of himself."
